prepare yourself for a sleepless night  it is likely to be the hottest on record  according to the met office temperatures  may not drop below 24 degrees in london  and the south of england and further  north in places like manchester or  sheffield the overnight minimum could be  25 now 25 degrees normally defines a hot  day in the uk and it would smash the  current nighttime record of 23.9 degrees  set in august 1990 in brighton  these tropical nights when temperatures  stay above 20 degrees are becoming more  common although the met office isn't  sure yet there is a consistent trend  in the five years to 1975 the uk had six  tropical nights across an area of just  over 550 square miles that rose to 16  nights in the five years to 1995  affecting nearly 2 000 square miles and  in the first years of this century there  were 20 nights affecting an area across  eight and a half thousand square miles  now a lot of the focus in the heatwave  is understandably on the daytime high  but there's growing evidence that it's  overnight temperatures that are more  closely tied to deaths  during the day your heart is put under  strain as it pumps extra blood to the  skin to keep cool if you haven't drunk  enough water the blood can become sticky  increasing the risk of clots and with  temperatures high at night as well the  body never gets a chance to recover with  cumulative physiological stress on vital  organs the elderly are at highest risk  now a huge area of western europe is  enduring ferocious heat at the moment as  a result of a plume of air swept north  from africa more than 1 000 people in  spain and portugal have so far died and  while we talk about this as being  unprecedented now by the end of the  century the met office predicts that  temperatures will top 40 degrees in the  uk somewhere between every 3 to 15 years  depending on greenhouse gas emissions  climate change is making heat waves more  likely and more intense and scientists  say the quicker we reach net zero the  better
 

